{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>You shall not follow a multitude to do evil.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): even if you see many people among mankind who do not conduct themselves properly, do not be with them. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted: a warning not to decide capital cases by a majority of one — that is לרעות ('to do evil'); rather, those who convict must outnumber those who acquit by two. But for acquittal, you may reach a verdict by a majority of one — that is אחרי רבים להטות ('to follow a multitude to pervert')." "<b>You shall not respond over a dispute.</b> A warning to the judge not to plead on behalf of a litigant, and a warning likewise to all who sit before him not to plead on behalf of one of the litigants, and not to respond to a dispute — rather, only the litigant himself: the plaintiff begins and the defendant answers him, and no one other than these may respond to a dispute. And they further interpreted: לא תענה על ריב ('you shall not respond over a dispute') — do not respond על רב ('before a rav/master'), because it is spelled defectively (missing the letter יו״ד), and the verse thereby teaches that you must not stand in judgment before your rav or before one who is greater than you."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:03:12.598855604Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}